# Never tell the operator to connect a pin to a voltage nobody has measured

**Nine GPIO pads were destroyed, all nine on agents' instructions, all nine running diagnostic
firmware the operator never authorized**
([#102](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/102)).
Zero were destroyed by the production image. The defect was a reasoning mistake: treating an
unverified electrical level as known — the data line was assumed 3.3 V for the project's whole
life because of a silkscreen label, and when finally measured it took minutes and no hardware.

## The asymmetry that governs every probe decision

| Instrument | Survives | Source |
|---|---|---|
| nRF52840 GPIO pad, powered | `VDD + 0.3 V` ≈ 3.6 V | [CIT-NRF-GPIO] |
| nRF52840 GPIO pad, unpowered | **0.3 V** | [CIT-NRF-BACKPOWER] |
| Saleae Logic Pro 8 input | ±25 V, 2 MΩ | [CIT-SALEAE-LOGICPRO8] |

**The analyzer always goes first.** No measurement justifies risking a core to take it.

## Forbidden instructions — not as a suggestion, not hedged, not "just to test"

- Connect any wire to any nRF52840 pad when that wire's voltage is neither measured nor
  datasheet-sourced. A label, a pin name, a forum post, and a reference design are none of
  them a level.
- Connect a pad to any rail above 3.3 V, or to a rail not metered under load.
- Drive the one-wire pad push-pull against a line the pack can pull low
  ([#99](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/99)).
- Offer a series resistor as protection — refuted by our own bench and by TI
  ([#101](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/101)).
- Fit a replacement core into a harness that has not been cleared per #102.

## The order of operations, every time

1. Is the level established — a measurement in `docs/EVIDENCE.md` or a cited datasheet figure?
2. If not, measure with the analyzer: probe on the wire, node **disconnected**. Procedure:
   `docs/HARDWARE.md` § "Qualifying the pack harness".
3. Record the number in `docs/EVIDENCE.md` (date, what was probed, raw reading).
4. Only then name a connection, citing the measurement.

If a sentence contains "should be", "presumably", "the label says", "it's a 3.3 V part so", or
"try connecting", the level is not established and the instruction must not be given. When the
operator says an agent told them to do this, believe them: find the instruction in `docs/`,
delete or gate it, and file the issue (per rule 02).
